WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.554
This is the exception desk, a Selerim logistics concept built with fictional data.

00:00:05.554 --> 00:00:14.954
The problem is familiar: shipping documents arrive in different places, people match them by hand, and missing paperwork delays the next step.

00:00:14.954 --> 00:00:20.508
This guided tour uses captured states from the working application and synthetic narration.

00:00:20.508 --> 00:00:24.781
The current run uses deterministic PDF extraction and template drafts.

00:00:24.781 --> 00:00:32.898
It is not a live language model demonstration, and none of the names, shipments, or recipients represent real clients.

00:00:34.398 --> 00:00:38.062
The fixture contains fifty shipment records and forty documents.

00:00:38.062 --> 00:00:40.911
Here, shipment eight has a weight discrepancy.

00:00:40.911 --> 00:00:48.645
The document says three thousand two hundred fifteen pounds, while the shipment record says two thousand seven hundred fifteen.

00:00:48.645 --> 00:00:53.123
Both values appear together so a reviewer can understand the exception.

00:00:53.123 --> 00:00:58.008
The source panel shows extracted fields and links to the original PDF.

00:00:58.008 --> 00:01:01.671
The system uses exact shipment references within the job.

00:01:01.671 --> 00:01:06.149
Missing or unknown references go to review instead of being guessed.

00:01:07.649 --> 00:01:12.874
The follow-up draft names the detected issue and asks for corrected or missing paperwork.

00:01:12.874 --> 00:01:16.606
A person reviews the evidence and chooses approve or reject.

00:01:16.606 --> 00:01:23.324
In this captured state, the reviewer has approved the draft, and a separate send action is now available.

00:01:23.324 --> 00:01:25.563
Approval does not itself send anything.

00:01:25.563 --> 00:01:31.162
That distinction is enforced by the server, not only by the appearance of a button.

00:01:31.162 --> 00:01:37.507
The acceptance tests also check that rejected drafts cannot be sent and changed evidence invalidates old approval.

00:01:39.007 --> 00:01:42.226
The reviewer then chooses send to demo outbox.

00:01:42.226 --> 00:01:49.066
The application records the state change and shows that the draft was sent to the local simulation.

00:01:49.066 --> 00:01:54.297
There is no SMTP connection and no external email API behind this action.

00:01:54.297 --> 00:01:56.712
All recipients use a non-deliverable domain.

00:01:56.712 --> 00:02:04.759
This deliberately narrow transport makes the concept safe to demonstrate while still exercising the actual approval and sending state machine.

00:02:04.759 --> 00:02:08.783
Repeating the same send does not create another outbox entry.

00:02:10.283 --> 00:02:17.556
The outbox preserves the approved recipient and message so the reviewer can inspect what the simulated action contained.

00:02:17.556 --> 00:02:22.404
This is fictional evidence, not a client result or a production integration.

00:02:22.404 --> 00:02:30.485
The system reads source records without changing them, limits file access to the current job, and logs its business actions.

00:02:30.485 --> 00:02:34.526
The model adapter, when separately configured, can help with wording.

00:02:34.526 --> 00:02:37.354
It has no approval or sending tool.

00:02:37.354 --> 00:02:41.394
This recorded run remains in the explicitly labeled template mode.

00:02:42.894 --> 00:02:48.461
The activity log connects the source, draft, human decision, and simulated send.

00:02:48.461 --> 00:02:54.956
Its local hash chain is verifiable, although a database owner could defeat those controls.

00:02:54.956 --> 00:03:00.524
On the actual fixture parser run, all thirty clean documents matched correctly.

00:03:00.524 --> 00:03:07.946
All eleven planted document findings and sixty five missing-document gaps were detected, with no false positives.

00:03:07.946 --> 00:03:14.441
These are deterministic fixture results, not a promise about arbitrary scans or real operations.

00:03:14.441 --> 00:03:22.792
The next production step is a scoped audit of one workflow, its data, security requirements, and acceptance criteria.
